{
  "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
  "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the most important point that I can make today is that this is a case about the substantive requirements of antitrust law, and just as in Dura and in Blue Chip Stamps, the Court articulated the substantive requirements for pleading a claim under the securities law, and just as in Anza, it did so under RICO, so too in Associated General Contractors, in Trinko. And in the instant case, the Court is faced with the question of what a plaintiff needs to plead in order to state a claim and show an entitlement to relief under the antitrust laws."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "plaintiffs' allegation that there is a contract combination or conspiracy in restraint of trade. The complaint states, and I quote, \"in the absence of any meaningful competition among the defendants,\" and, quote, in light of the parallel course of conduct that each engaged in to prevent competition, the plaintiffs -- the defendants conspired."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice STEVENS",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, but isn't the next sentence, the substance of the sentence is \"plaintiffs allege upon information and belief that defendants have entered into a contract combination or conspiracy to prevent competitive entry in their respective telephone and/or high speed interstate markets, and agreed not to compete with one another and otherwise allocated customers and markets to one another.\" Now, does that state a violation of the Sherman Act?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice STEVENS",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Of course, that may be true on summary judgment, you may be dead right on the merits, but are you telling me that an allegation that the defendants have agreed not to compete with one another is not a statement of fact?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice STEVENS",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, what if they said they agreed in writing not to compete with one another, would that be sufficient? Or if they have agreed orally not to compete with one another, would that be sufficient?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GINSBURG",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Wasn't that a summary judgment case and hadn't there been discovery before? The Matsushita decision?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KENNEDY",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So in the negligently drove case, the plaintiff negligently drove over -- the defendant negligently drove over the plaintiff, if it's not specific as to time and place it must be dismissed? If it's specific as to time and place it's, it withstands the motion?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0009",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The fundamental concern of the United States is that the decision of the Second Circuit can be read to hold that a Section 1 Sherman Act complaint will survive a motion to dismiss merely by alleging parallel action or inaction in attaching the bare assertion of an agreement. Such a result fails to appreciate that parallel action or inaction is ubiquitous in our economy and often reflects beneficial competitive forces."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SOUTER",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0010",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, is that really what they -- I thought, and correct me if I'm wrong, but I thought that the, that the Court spoke of no set of facts, only on the assumption that there had been a pleading which did raise a plausible, possible inference of forbidden conduct, and I thought the Court was saying if the, if the plausibility criterion has been satisfied, then the only way that the defendant can get a dismissal is by showing that there is no set of facts which would actually support the action."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SOUTER",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0000-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So, so that if plausibility is the standard this does not meet the standard of plausibility, that's your argument?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Should a plaintiffs's complaint fail because it includes unnecessary, verbose, admissible evidence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. BARNETT",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BARNETT: No. It should fail if it fail -- if it does not allege facts that indicate reasonable found --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: But what if it didn't? I mean, I mean face the question that Justice Stevens puts. Suppose you have a complaint that says nothing else except that these defendants entered into an agreement in -- in restraint of trade."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "COURT",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF J. DOUGLAS RICHARDS ON BEHALF OF RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: You mean the mere fact that you have alleged something is against their self-interest is enough to make an issue of fact on whether it's against their self-interest?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. RICHARDS",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0002-0001",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RICHARDS: I understand that. But we have alleged that as fact, Justice Ginsburg, and that fact and that allegation has to be treated as true under conventional pleading standards for purposes of a motion to dismiss."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I'd also like a clear answer, and I would like to go back to Justice Stevens' question because I'm not sure what you're thinking there."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. RICHARDS",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RICHARDS: I think that that would satisfy conventional pleading standards under Rule 8(a). On the other hand, I don't think it would satisfy the Second Circuit's standard below, because the Second Circuit required enough facts to enable a court to wrap its mind around a complainant, understanding what it is you claimed happened."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: But if they don't do that, is it your argument that simply by behaving differently from the way Congress assumed when it passed the statute, that raises the plausible inference of violation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: You have to say what it is, that's all, and even if it's implausible?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What if you pled more than you had to, and it's clear from what you pled that you were drawing an implausible inference? Can't the complaint then be dismissed for failure to state a claim?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1126",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0c7848947fb470e71784dfb538b3ffe240400f5fd4b675ce90a590d148dbb5cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0c7848947fb4-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, Mr. Kellogg. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
